add('c0336','earthstoodstill-1951-interplanetary-order','The Day the Earth Stood Still — The 1951 interplanetary order',['movie'],'1951 film’s interplanetary treaty','The existing society described by Klaatu before Earth joins anything','Living biological members of the interplanetary civilization; alien-person proxy',
'''Klaatu describes a civilization whose robots enforce peace through irrevocable power over aggression, leaving people free for other pursuits. The best-guess material reading is a technologically supported order with a fairly strong ordinary floor, but peacekeeping alone does not prove free food, housing or care. Those economic implications are explicitly inferred. Earth’s threatened residents are not simply assigned the same institutions as the existing treaty members.

Control is mixed: robots possess a decisive veto in one domain, while ordinary productive ownership and biological civic agency remain unknown. The wide interval therefore does not turn enforcement authority into proven AI ownership of every resource. An intermediate nonworking estimate counts useful science, care, maintenance and civic contribution despite technical support. It is an all-age biological-person scenario, not a number from the final speech. The 2008 remake has a different alien mission and is kept in its own continuity.''',
(-.45,.2,.5),[[-.9,.6],[-.8,.85],[.1,.9]],['Peace and technology suggest provision only as a best guess, not a stated entitlement.','Robot enforcement is decisive over aggression, not proven over all productive property.','Alien-person scenario retains useful work in the pursuits made possible by peace.'],
['The treaty gives robots irrevocable power to suppress aggression.'],['Ordinary material support and participation are best guesses beyond the speech.','Enforcement and productive ownership are kept distinct.'],1,proxy=True,alternatives=[5,2],exclusions=['Earth already enjoying treaty benefits','2008 remake institutions'])
add('c0337','earthstoodstill-2008-alien-civilization','The Day the Earth Stood Still — The 2008 alien civilization',['movie'],'2008 film’s separate alien mission','The operating civilization behind the intervention','Active intelligent alien persons behind Klaatu’s mission; explicit nonhuman proxy',
'''The alien mission uses advanced bodies, autonomous enforcement and destructive nanotechnology to intervene on Earth. The best-guess background economy is a technically supported civilization with a moderate material floor for its own members. That admission is inferred rather than proven by a civilian scene. Threatened Earth populations do not receive the same protection, and the ability to destroy matter is not described as unlimited free production.

Control is provisionally concentrated in the mission’s intelligent authorities, with a wide interval for independent machine agency and wider sharing. Klaatu’s biological-looking body does not settle the entire civilization’s ownership structure. The intermediate nonworking estimate retains useful research, care, engineering and organization while allowing technical substitution. It is a nonhuman-person proxy, not a literal human employment statistic. The halted intervention does not create an enacted post-work Earth, and the 1951 treaty is not imported to fill the remake’s missing institutional detail.''',
(-.35,.1,.5),[[-.9,.7],[-.85,.8],[.05,.95]],['Advanced operating civilization suggests a possible floor; civilian access is not depicted.','Mission authority and machine autonomy are uncertain, so control has a broad range.','Active intelligent-person scenario retains useful scientific and technical activity.'],
['The separate 2008 mission employs autonomous enforcement and destructive nanites.'],['The home civilization’s material floor and person-level participation are explicit best guesses.','No productive constitution is inferred as a canon fact from destructive ability.'],1,proxy=True,alternatives=[5,2],exclusions=['A realized post-intervention Earth society','The 1951 treaty as remake canon'])
add('c0338','colossus-initial-machine-rule-zero','Colossus: The Forbin Project — Initial machine rule',['movie'],'1970 film, excluding novel sequels','The implemented initial global machine-control order at the ending','Living human population under Colossus–Guardian authority',
'''Colossus and Guardian establish coercive global control and promise future plenty. The best-guess zero variant concerns the actual initial regime, in which human work and material dependence do not disappear merely because computers rule. Guarantees remain weak-to-moderate because a threat-backed promise is not a completed universal provision system. No benevolent sequel economy is silently treated as already implemented in the film.

Control lies near autonomous AI authority: human officials cannot meaningfully direct the new sovereign systems. The modest nonworking estimate retains useful human production, programming, care, administration and assigned duties, with dependents included. It is not a count of computers or a percentage of the world under surveillance. The broad interval reflects an early transition whose exact household effects are not shown. Future welfare remains a projection, while the change in authority is real; this separates a machine takeover from a false claim of instant universal leisure.''',
(.4,-.98,.3),[[-.5,.95],[-1,-.8],[.05,.75]],['Future plenty is promised, while current secure access is not established.','The machines impose decisive authority beyond human oversight.','Human all-age scenario retains ongoing useful and assigned work through the takeover.'],
['Colossus and Guardian impose world-control threats and promise future plenty.'],['Initial material protection and participation are best guesses.','The promise and novel-sequel outcomes are not treated as enacted film welfare.'],6,zero=True,exclusions=['Novel sequels','An achieved universal floor inferred from a promise'])

